Abstract
Organizations increasingly confront a threat in remote hiring: the ‘phantom candidate’, a synthetic persona created using artificial intelligence by hostile actors who pose as legitimate applicants to infiltrate organizations through remote hiring processes and secure employment as remote workers. State-sponsored phantom candidates leverage deepfake technology for video interviews, AI-generated credentials, and fabricated digital identities to secure organizational access, posing security and operational risks. Drawing upon personnel assessment validity frameworks and high-reliability organizing perspectives, we develop a framework centered on four detection strategies: (1) designing assessment complexity that exposes AI consistency failures across time and contexts; (2) implementing multi-modal verification that exploits AI coordination limitations across independent channels; (3) building organizational vigilance through systematic threat intelligence and cross-functional learning; and (4) integrating human judgment with automated detection to leverage complementary capabilities. The framework addresses the ontological validity challenge posed when selection systems assess entirely fabricated personas rather than authentic candidates. This requires explicit verification of the foundational assumption that a human referent exists whose performance can be predicted. The framework also provides practitioners with evidence-based methods for maintaining hiring integrity while preserving assessment validity in an era of sophisticated digital deception.

