1. Introduction
The global shift toward remote work has altered the architecture of personnel selection (Van Iddekinge, Arnold, Aguinis, Lang, & Lievens, 2023). By increasingly decoupling assessment from physical co-presence, organizations have gained increased access to diverse talent but have also weakened the implicit, long-standing safeguards of identity verification. The resulting structural change has created a systemic vulnerability, now exploited by a concerning new phenomenon: the deployment of the ‘phantom candidate’—a synthetic persona engineered with artificial intelligence by hostile actors posing as legitimate candidates to bypass remote vetting procedures and secure employment through remote hiring processes (Son, 2025).
Reports from the U.S. Federal Bureau of Investigation now explicitly warn of threat actors deploying deepfake technologies and stolen personally identifiable information to successfully pass remote video interviews, securing employment in sensitive sectors to exfiltrate data and intellectual property (FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center, 2022). Cybersecurity analyses document sophisticated, ongoing campaigns where phantom employees, hired through deceptive means, have caused millions of dollars in financial losses and established persistent security vulnerabilities within hundreds of organizations (Heichler, 2025). Legal advisories report that over 300 U.S. companies have inadvertently hired fraudulent candidates, with some maintaining organizational access for 4–6 months before detection (Alston & Bird, 2025). Government documentation reveals systematic complaints involving “voice spoofing, or potentially voice deepfakes” during online interviews where “actions and lip movement of the person seen on-camera do not completely coordinate with the audio of the person speaking” (FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center, 2022). The coordinated, state-linked nature of these schemes documented across multiple jurisdictions demonstrates that synthetic identity fraud represents organized rather than opportunistic activity.
Personnel assessment validity, even under its current unitary formulation, presupposes a foundational condition that is rarely tested explicitly: that assessment responses arise from the cognitive and behavioral processes of an authentic human candidate, whose future job performance can be predicted from those processes (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018; cf. Messick, 1995). Physical co-presence in traditional hiring provided implicit authentication—when candidates appeared in person for interviews or submitted handwritten applications, organizations could reasonably assume they were assessing actual human beings. Remote assessment has eliminated this implicit safeguard, while the response-process assumption underlying validity arguments has not been adapted to require explicit verification. Synthetic identity fraud violates this prerequisite by introducing entities that can generate assessment responses but possess no actual knowledge, skills, or abilities to measure and no future job performance to predict. Our framework addresses this challenge by positioning authenticity verification as a prerequisite control—analogous to identity authentication protocols in high-stakes standardized testing such as the GRE or GMAT—that must be established before personnel assessment validity frameworks can proceed to evaluate whether assessments accurately measure job-relevant competencies and predict performance outcomes (Cizek & Wollack, 2016).
Malicious synthetic identity fraud also differs substantially in intent, motivation, and potential consequences from traditional applicant deception, thereby requiring reassessment of the validity arguments that underlie personnel assessment (AERA et al., 2014; Ho, Powell, & Stanley, 2021; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018). The appearance of the phantom candidate poses an ontological problem for the science of personnel selection (Sackett, Lievens, Van Iddekinge, & Kuncel, 2017; Tippins, Oswald, & McPhail, 2021). The challenge is not merely one of measurement error, but of the nonexistence of the entity to be measured. The scientific legitimacy of any selection procedure rests upon its position within a nomological network—the system of established relationships connecting a latent construct (e.g., an individual's cognitive ability), its operationalization (e.g., a test score), and theoretically related outcomes (e.g., job performance) (Binning & Barrett, 1989; Kane, 2013). The phantom candidate severs the foundational link in this chain. When an assessment prompt is processed not by a human mind drawing upon authentic experience but by an algorithmic proxy, the resulting output is not an indicator of a latent human trait. Instead, the relationship is between a query and a probabilistic textual or visual generation. The inferential basis of personnel selection science, which depends on predicting future human behavior from present human characteristics, is rendered spurious in these encounters. Traditional frameworks are predicated on the telltale signs of human dishonesty—increased cognitive load, emotional leakage, or behavioral over-compensation that arise when humans attempt to deceive (DePaulo et al., 2003; Vrij, Hartwig, & Granhag, 2019).
Generative artificial intelligence, while remarkably capable, is not an infallible system. AI operates on principles that create specific, documented, and predictable constraints. Technical research confirms that probabilistic reasoning results in demonstrable failures in maintaining logical and narrative consistency when queried across different contexts or over time (Mündler et al., 2023). Furthermore, the computational difficulty of coordinating separate generative models for language, audio, and video results in detectable artifacts and synchronization failures during real-time, multi-modal interaction (Shao, Wu, & Liu, 2023). These are not random glitches but inherent properties of the technology's architecture, providing a stable, evidence-based foundation for intervention.
Drawing upon personnel assessment validity frameworks (Schmitt & Chan, 1998; Tippins et al., 2021) and high-reliability organizing perspectives (Weick & Sutcliffe, 2015), we develop a framework that synthesizes insights from multiple theoretical domains into four actionable pillars. First, we propose tactical strategies of assessment design complexity and verification diversity to systematically probe and expose the identified architectural weaknesses of AI systems. Second, to address the high-consequence, uncertain nature of the phantom candidate threat, we draw on high-reliability organizing principles (Weick & Sutcliffe, 2015) to conceptualize the cultural and procedural posture required for managing complex, failure-intolerant systems. Finally, we propose the necessity of socio-technical integration for implementation, specifying how to formally augment human contextual judgment with the technical detection capabilities of automated tools.
We begin by reviewing the theoretical underpinnings of personnel assessment validity and high-reliability organizing to further delineate the conceptual space our framework occupies and the specific gaps it aims to fill. We then articulate four formal research propositions, detailing the causal mechanisms and empirical evidence for each pillar of the framework. We conclude by exploring the implications for human resource management theory and practice, addressing critical implementation and ethical challenges, and outlining a comprehensive research agenda to equip organizations for an evolving era of digital hiring.
2. Literature review and theoretical background
2.1. Phenomenological background
Synthetic identity creation involves creating entirely fictitious candidate personas using deepfakes for realistic video/audio representations and real-time facial mapping during interviews (Öhman, 2022; Uittenhove, Shahreza, Marcel, & Ramon, 2025). Deepfake technology in hiring contexts uses generative video and audio manipulation to create synthetic interview participants, mapping facial expressions and voice patterns onto fabricated identities or real individuals (Ajder, Patrini, Cavalli, & Cullen, 2019). Generative Adversarial Networks produce photorealistic profile pictures and fabricate complete digital footprints, including social media profiles and portfolio websites (Goodfellow, 2014). Verification evasion uses AI to create fraudulent documents (diplomas, certificates, passports, IDs) increasingly difficult to distinguish from legitimate ones, potentially bypassing human and automated checks (Mirsky & Lee, 2021). AI can also circumvent digital verification processes by identifying security weaknesses or generating data patterns to fool detection algorithms, as seen in North Korean IT workers using stolen American identities with AI-enhanced masking (Department of Justice, 2025; Son, 2025). Motivations range from financial gain through unqualified employment to corporate espionage, data theft, and inserting malicious actors into sensitive positions. The U.S. Department of Justice documented cases where fraudulent employees diverted wages to fund illicit programs (Department of Justice, 2025). Cybersecurity, financial services, and technology sectors report the highest incidence rates due to their sensitive data and systems. Interview response augmentation employs AI tools to enhance candidates' performance during remote assessments (Tschang & Almirall, 2021).
Our framework focuses specifically on malicious synthetic identity fraud—scenarios where entirely fabricated personas interact with selection systems and no authentic human candidate exists. This distinction matters because when real candidates use AI assistance for application enhancement, traditional validity frameworks remain applicable: an authentic human referent exists whose actual capabilities correlate with assessment performance and whose future job performance can be predicted from present characteristics (Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018; Tippins et al., 2021). The phantom candidate threat eliminates this foundational assumption by severing the link between assessment responses and human competencies. The technologies documented above—deepfakes for realistic video interviews, generative AI for fabricated credentials, identity verification evasion through algorithmic exploitation—enable this qualitative escalation from human deception (where actual capabilities are misrepresented) to complete fabrication (where no authentic capabilities exist to assess).
2.2. Personnel assessment validity frameworks: Core principles and contemporary challenges
Personnel selection rests on a body of validity theory that has evolved substantially since the mid-twentieth-century conceptualization of validity as three discrete types: content, criterion-related, and construct. The current consensus, codified in the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (AERA et al., 2014) and the Principles for the Validation and Use of Personnel Selection Procedures (Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018, p. 4), treats validity as a unitary concept—a single overarching judgment about the degree to which accumulated evidence and theory support the proposed interpretation and use of test scores. What were once described as distinct types of validity are now understood as different sources of evidence bearing on this single argument. The Standards identify five such sources: test content, response processes, internal structure, relations to other variables, and consequences of testing (AERA et al., 2014, pp. 26–31). For personnel selection, the resulting validity argument concerns whether scores support accurate inferences about job-relevant knowledge, skills, abilities, and other characteristics (KSAOs) and predict subsequent work behavior (Sackett, Zhang, Berry, & Lievens, 2022; Schmitt & Chan, 1998; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018; Tippins et al., 2021).
The phantom candidate threat is consequential precisely because it disrupts this validity argument at its foundation, although it does not implicate all five sources equally. Evidence based on response processes is the source most directly violated: any score interpretation assumes that responses were produced by the cognitive and behavioral processes the procedure was designed to elicit (AERA et al., 2014; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018), an assumption that fails when a synthetic persona generates responses algorithmically rather than through human cognition. Content-related evidence is implicated in turn, because content-based validation strategies assume that the entity engaging with assessment content is the same entity whose KSAOs are being inferred. Evidence based on relations to other variables—what was traditionally termed criterion-related validity—is undermined downstream, because predictor–criterion linkages established in samples of authentic candidates cannot be expected to hold for synthetic respondents whose responses index no actual human capability and for whom no future job performance exists to predict (Sackett et al., 2022). Internal-structure and consequences-of-testing evidence are less directly affected, though the consequences source becomes relevant when fraudulent hires generate downstream organizational harm.
Two distinctions organize the analysis that follows. The first separates the digital assessment context, which introduces validity threats common to all remote candidates (e.g., construct-irrelevant variance from technological proficiency or environmental conditions; Berkelaar & Buzzanell, 2015), from the malicious synthetic identity threat, which violates the more fundamental assumption that an authentic human candidate exists at all. The second is the distinction between validity evidence—the substantive argument supporting a proposed score interpretation (Kane, 2013; Messick, 1989)—and prerequisite controls on score provenance, such as the identity-authentication procedures used by testing organizations like ETS and GMAC (Educational Testing Service, n.d.; Graduate Management Admission Council, n.d.). On this view, such controls must be satisfied before validity evidence can meaningfully be evaluated, because the validity argument presupposes that the scored responses were produced by the identified test taker under standardized conditions (American Educational Research Association [AERA] et al., 2014).
2.2.1. Digital assessment and validity threats
Digital selection environments create validity challenges that increase organizational vulnerability. Goretzko and Israel (2022) and König and Langer (2022) argue that digital environments create challenges requiring theoretical attention, identifying problems where algorithmic scoring lacks transparency, making construct validity evaluation difficult. Hickman et al. (2022) extend analysis to machine learning-based selection, identifying algorithmic fairness concerns and changing data conditions that threaten validity over time.
Brymer, Paraskevas, Josefy, and Ellram (2024) acknowledge dynamic information exchanges in pipeline hiring, arguing that sustained candidate-organization interactions reduce information asymmetries over time. Their empirical analysis of college football recruiting demonstrates that extended information exchange improves hiring outcomes when involving authentic candidate information. However, their model explicitly assumes information authenticity—an assumption that synthetic identity fraud violates. The information processing model's effectiveness depends on signal quality and interpretation accuracy. Basch and Melchers (2021) demonstrate that technology-mediated interviews enable new impression management tactics where raters may misattribute a candidate's smooth answers to actual interview skills and qualifications, whereas responses might simply stem from the use of notes.
2.2.2. Malicious synthetic identity fraud: Violation of foundational authenticity assumptions
Applicant misrepresentation is among the most extensively studied threats to selection validity, and the cumulative literature provides a useful baseline against which the synthetic identity threat can be characterized. Meta-analytic evidence documents that applicants regularly inflate self-presentations on personality and other self-report measures, with within-subjects effect sizes typically in the moderate-to-large range, and that this faking distorts score distributions and rank orders even when predictive utility is not fully eliminated (Birkeland, Manson, Kisamore, Brannick, & Smith, 2006; Hu & Connelly, 2021; Salgado, 2016). Fletcher and Trautrims (2024) analyze this behavior as an information-asymmetry problem in which candidates possess private information about their qualifications and strategically present themselves favorably. Across these treatments a common assumption holds: the candidate is a real person whose misrepresentations bear some relation, however distorted, to actual underlying capabilities. Selection science remains workable in this regime because a human referent exists whose true KSAOs and future job performance can in principle be estimated and predicted, even if the available evidence is noisy.
Recent work on candidate use of generative AI in selection occupies an intermediate position on this spectrum and illustrates where the prior literature begins to strain. Lievens and Dunlop (2025) argue against treating all applicant GenAI use as inherently disruptive, noting that GenAI assistance can sometimes enhance rather than substitute for authentic candidate capability and may reduce certain forms of construct-irrelevant variance. Companion work has nonetheless raised serious concerns about construct- and criterion-related validity evidence when GenAI substantially substitutes for candidate cognition (Holtrop & Bronzwaer, 2025; Mirowska, 2025), and recent applied studies show that warnings can reduce, but do not eliminate, self-reported GenAI assistance during unproctored assessments (Robie, Wingate, Baytalskaya, & Butera, 2026). What unites these treatments—and what distinguishes them from the threat we address—is that they continue to assume an authentic human candidate behind the application. The validity question is whether the score still indexes that candidate's KSAOs.
Malicious synthetic identity fraud removes this assumption. As documented in Section 2.1 and corroborated by federal advisories and prosecutions (Department of Justice, 2025; FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center, 2022), threat actors now construct end-to-end fabricated personas using deepfake video and audio, AI-generated credentials, and synthesized digital footprints, with the explicit goal of being hired into remote roles. Unlike a faking applicant or an AI-coached one, a phantom candidate is not a person whose self-presentation is distorted; there is no person at all whose KSAOs the assessment could over- or underestimate. The score, the interview behaviors, the credential package, and the work-sample output are produced by coordinated generative systems in service of an external operator whose interests are unrelated to the role.
This distinction has direct consequences for the unitary validity argument established in Section 2.2. Validity rests on accumulated evidence that scores can be interpreted as the test developer or user intends, and the Standards explicitly identify response processes as a foundational source of evidence (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014, pp. 26–31; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018). When responses are produced by algorithmic generation rather than the cognitive and behavioral processes the procedure was designed to elicit, the response-process assumption is not merely strained but violated. Content evidence and evidence based on relations to other variables are then undermined as a consequence, because both presuppose that an authentic human candidate is engaging with the content and that future job performance exists to be predicted. The inferential chain on which personnel selection science depends—from present human characteristics to future human behavior—is severed not through measurement error but through the absence of the entity to be measured. Re-establishing this inferential chain requires a control that operates upstream of the validity argument itself, which is the subject of Section 2.2.3.
2.2.3. Authenticity verification as prerequisite control: Precedents from high-stakes assessment
The distinction between authenticity verification as a prerequisite control versus an embedded measurement component has direct precedent in high-stakes testing contexts where substantial consequences attach to score interpretations. Standardized examinations for graduate admissions (GRE, GMAT) and professional licensure treat identity authentication as foundational security controls that must be satisfied before psychometric properties of scores can be meaningfully interpreted. The Educational Testing Service implements comprehensive authentication protocols for the GRE General Test, including biometric facial recognition that compares test-takers to identification documents, artificial intelligence verification that randomly confirms identity match multiple times throughout the examination, and palm-vein scanning technology at testing centers. The Graduate Management Admission Council employs similar authentication infrastructure for the GMAT, utilizing approved identification validation, live check-in via human proctors before examination commencement, continuous monitoring through webcam and microphone during testing, and post-examination forensic analysis to identify authentication failures (ETS, 2024; GMAC, 2024). These security measures are conceptualized not as additional validity evidence for the assessment instruments themselves, but rather as prerequisite controls ensuring that scores originate from authenticated test-takers. Test security threats—including proxy testing, impersonation, and identity fraud—are classified as factors that undermine score meaning by violating assumptions about score provenance rather than as psychometric deficiencies in the instruments. The standards emphasize that test users bear responsibility for implementing authentication controls appropriate to their assessment contexts, with more stringent controls required when scores inform high-stakes decisions.
We adopt this same conceptualization for personnel selection under synthetic identity threats. Authenticity verification operates as a foundational control that establishes whether the entity being assessed is an authentic human candidate whose performance can be predicted. This prerequisite must be satisfied before the unitary validity argument and the reliability requirements that support it can be meaningfully evaluated for whether assessments accurately measure job-relevant KSAOs and predict job performance. Unlike high-stakes standardized testing, where authentication occurs at centralized testing facilities with specialized equipment, remote hiring contexts require organizations to implement authenticity verification through assessment design strategies that exploit documented AI technical limitations. The quantified predictions throughout our framework (e.g., multiplicative detection advantages, percentage accuracy improvements, time reduction estimates) are presented as illustrative examples demonstrating theoretical principles rather than empirically validated performance guarantees. Our four propositions specify detection strategies that function as authenticity verification controls, enabling organizations to establish this foundational prerequisite before proceeding with validity-focused selection decisions.
2.3. High-reliability organizing perspectives for personnel selection under phantom candidate threats
Personnel assessment validity frameworks identify what must be protected—the multiple sources of validity evidence and reliability requirements that support the validity argument for a selection system—but do not specify how organizations should structure themselves to detect and respond to systematic threats that target these requirements. Phantom candidates represent low-probability, high-consequence failures where individual hiring mistakes create systemic organizational vulnerabilities through insider access. Traditional human resource management practices treat verification as a routine administrative process rather than as a systematic threat management requiring organizational capabilities that span HR, cybersecurity, legal, and technical functions. High-reliability organizing principles address how organizations can maintain critical system functions when operating in environments where failure consequences exceed normal tolerance levels and threats evolve through sophisticated, adaptive mechanisms (Weick & Sutcliffe, 2015). The principles translate directly to maintaining hiring integrity: constant questioning of verification adequacy (preoccupation with failure), refusing to treat verification as checklist compliance (reluctance to simplify), and integrating specialized knowledge across functions because phantom candidate detection requires psychological assessment expertise, technical analysis capabilities, and legal investigation competencies that exceed traditional HR boundaries (deference to expertise).
Lee (2021) demonstrates that cybersecurity threats create high-consequence, low-probability scenarios requiring organizational mindfulness and systematic vigilance, revealing direct relevance to hiring security challenges where phantom candidates exploit organizational assumptions about candidate authenticity. The translation establishes that personnel selection under fraud conditions shares critical characteristics with systems traditionally addressed through high-reliability organizing: consequences of failure are severe and potentially catastrophic, threats evolve through sophisticated technical capabilities that exploit organizational assumptions, and effective response requires integrating expertise across multiple specialized domains (Nixon & Kerr, 2011).
Preoccupation with failure requires constantly questioning assumptions about the effectiveness of security controls and searching for new vulnerabilities rather than assuming verification adequacy (Weick & Sutcliffe, 2015). Traditional hiring practices assume candidate authenticity unless obvious red flags appear, operating from a default position of trust. High-reliability organizing inverts this assumption under conditions of systematic threat: organizations actively search for evidence of authenticity rather than passively awaiting deception indicators. FBI documentation of technical indicators, including “voice spoofing” where “actions and lip movement of the person seen on-camera do not completely coordinate with the audio of the person speaking,” demonstrates the systematic nature of threats that organizations must actively monitor (FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center, 2022). Preoccupation with failure in this context means designing assessment processes that treat each verification point as a potential vulnerability requiring active confirmation rather than assumed reliability.
Reluctance to simplify interpretations addresses the organizational tendency to reduce complex threats to manageable categories or checklist protocols. High-reliability organizing principles require maintaining a deeper understanding of threat complexity and resisting pressure to reduce detection to algorithmic rules or standardized protocols that fraudsters can reverse-engineer (Engemann, 2018). Deference to expertise requires integrating specialized knowledge across organizational functions because phantom candidate detection involves domains that exceed any single function's competencies (Weick & Sutcliffe, 2015). Traditional personnel selection resides within human resource management, which possesses expertise in psychological assessment, interview design, and candidate evaluation. Phantom candidate detection additionally requires cybersecurity expertise to identify technical artifacts of AI synthesis, legal expertise to navigate privacy and compliance requirements during intensive verification, and information technology capabilities to implement multi-modal verification systems. Francis and Michas (2013) provide evidence from audit quality research that fraud detection effectiveness depends on systematic organizational capabilities rather than individual detector expertise, demonstrating that complex fraud requires coordinated specialist integration.
Cross-functional integration becomes operationally critical rather than merely aspirational when threats span multiple domains. HR professionals understand construct validity and assessment design, but may lack the technical knowledge to identify deepfake artifacts or AI-generated response patterns. Cybersecurity professionals can detect technical synthesis indicators but may not understand how these artifacts threaten criterion-related validity or what assessment modifications would expose coordination failures. Legal professionals understand compliance requirements but need HR and security input to design verification processes that satisfy legal constraints while maintaining detection effectiveness. High-reliability organizing provides frameworks for systematic integration: establishing standing cross-functional committees with explicit responsibility for hiring security, creating shared knowledge repositories where technical threat intelligence informs HR practice and assessment outcomes inform security monitoring, and developing joint training programs that build mutual understanding across specialized domains.
The integration of personnel assessment validity frameworks with high-reliability organizing perspectives creates the foundation for our four-proposition framework. Validity frameworks establish what must be maintained: response-process and content-related evidence supporting authentic KSAO measurement, evidence based on relations to other variables supporting predictive accuracy, and reliability supporting measurement consistency (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018; Zhang, Van Iddekinge, Ployhart, Arnold, & Jordan, 2023). High-reliability organizing specifies how organizations structure themselves to protect these validity requirements under systematic threat: preoccupation with failure drives continuous verification improvement, reluctance to simplify maintains nuanced threat understanding, and deference to expertise enables cross-functional integration. The four propositions that follow specify concrete mechanisms through which organizations can exploit AI technical limitations while maintaining these organizational capabilities.
2.4. Integrated framework: Selection integrity under synthetic identity threats
Our framework integrates the unitary view of personnel assessment validity (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018; Tippins et al., 2021) with high-reliability organizing perspectives (Weick & Sutcliffe, 2015) through a nested structure that addresses both what must be protected in personnel selection and how organizations protect it under systematic threat conditions.
The validity argument for a selection system specifies what effective selection requires: response-process, content-related, and criterion-related evidence supporting accurate KSAO measurement and predictive accuracy, together with reliability of measurement. Authenticity verification operates as a prerequisite control on this argument—established before, not as part of, the validity evaluation, analogous to identity authentication in high-stakes standardized testing (ETS, 2024; GMAC, 2024). High-reliability organizing perspectives specify how organizations protect the validity argument under systematic threat: maintaining preoccupation with failure rather than assuming verification adequacy, refusing to simplify threats into checklist protocols, and integrating specialized expertise across functions. The four propositions that follow specify concrete mechanisms implementing these principles, exploiting documented AI technical limitations while preserving the validity argument an organization is trying to make about its hires.
Fig. 1 displays this nested architecture, with authenticity verification as the foundational layer that the four propositions establish, the validity argument as the middle layer that authenticity verification protects, and high-reliability organizing as the cross-cutting design logic. The propositions are complementary rather than substitutable. Proposition 1 exploits AI consistency failures across time and contexts. Proposition 2 exploits coordination failures across independent verification modalities, generating multiplicative rather than additive detection advantages. Proposition 3 builds organizational learning capacity that adapts as fraud techniques evolve. Proposition 4 integrates human contextual judgment with automated technical analysis to leverage complementary strengths. Each is developed in turn in Section 3.
