3. Research propositions
3.1. Proposition 1: Assessment complexity
Authentic candidates possess integrated knowledge and experience that enable coherent responses across different contextual framings of the same underlying information. When describing work experiences, educational background, or technical competencies, authentic candidates draw from actual memory and lived experience rather than probabilistic pattern matching to contextual cues (Blackburn & Paterson, 2021). This experiential foundation enables them to maintain logical consistency even when questions approach the same topics through different analytical lenses. For example, when discussing a project management experience first through a “leadership challenges” frame and later through a “technical problem-solving” context, genuine candidates can integrate these perspectives coherently because they derive from the same underlying experience. Responses may vary in emphasis or detail but maintain logical consistency about fundamental facts, timelines, and outcomes. Under the unitary validity argument, this distinction speaks directly to evidence based on response processes (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014; Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology, 2018): an authentic candidate's responses arise from the integrated cognitive and experiential processes the assessment is designed to engage, whereas a phantom candidate's responses are produced by separate generative processes whose coherence must be externally coordinated.
Technical research documents specific AI consistency limitations that assessment processes can exploit. Mündler et al. (2023) demonstrate through their SelfCheckGPT benchmark that “sampling multiple responses from an LLM with the same prompt” reveals systematic inconsistencies that would not occur in genuine human reasoning about authentic experiences. An et al. (2024) demonstrate that LLM performance degrades significantly when models must access and use information located in the middle of their input context. Technical limitations in multi-modal synthesis compound these consistency challenges. Real-time deepfake generation creates systematic trade-offs between speed and quality that create detectable artifacts, including inconsistency between visual and audio modalities, such as mismatched lip movements and speech content (Lewis et al., 2020; Patel et al., 2023). Maintaining consistency across multiple communication channels—verbal responses, visual presentation, and behavioral demonstrations—exceeds current AI synthesis capabilities, particularly under interactive assessment conditions (Munikoti et al., 2026; Schulze Buschoff, Akata, Bethge, & Schulz, 2025).
Contextual reframing approaches present questions about the same experiences or competencies through different analytical frameworks. Initial questions might explore a candidate's technical project through an implementation focus (“What were the primary technical challenges?”), followed later by interpersonal dynamics exploration (“How did you manage stakeholder disagreements during this project?”), and subsequently by resource management analysis (“What budget or timeline constraints affected your approach?”). Authentic candidates can integrate these perspectives coherently, while AI systems may generate inconsistent details due to probabilistic pattern matching to different contextual cues (Zhou, Wang, & Chen, 2023).
Assessment designs can present technical scenarios requiring candidates to explain concepts verbally, diagram solutions visually, and demonstrate problem-solving approaches behaviorally. Technical research demonstrates that maintaining consistency across these modalities exceeds current AI synthesis capabilities, particularly during real-time interactive assessment (Baer, Waardenburg, & Huysman, 2025; Brown & Vaishampayan, 2025). Detailed elaboration sequences begin with high-level descriptions and progressively request more specific details about the same experiences. Initial questions might explore general project management experience, followed by specific technical implementation details, team dynamics, client interactions, and lessons learned. The progression creates multiple opportunities for inconsistency as AI systems generate increasingly specific details that may contradict earlier responses, while authentic candidates can provide coherent elaboration based on experience.
Phantom candidates should demonstrate higher rates of internal contradictions when responding to similar questions framed through different contexts. Contradictions should become more frequent as temporal separation increases between related questions, as AI systems lack the persistent memory integration that enables authentic consistency maintenance. AI-generated candidates should struggle particularly with follow-up questions requiring integration of previous responses with new information or contexts. When assessment processes introduce unexpected information and ask candidates to relate it to their stated experience, synthetic responses should reveal inconsistencies or implausible connections that genuine candidates would avoid through actual knowledge integration. Based on the above discussion, we propose:
Proposition 1

Assessment processes designed to systematically exploit AI consistency limitations through temporal separation and contextual variation of related questions will demonstrate significantly higher detection rates for malicious synthetic identity fraud.
3.2. Proposition 2: Multi-modal verification
Personnel selection increasingly employs multi-modal assessment combining verbal interviews, written evaluations, technical demonstrations, and behavioral observations (Hemamou, Guillon, Martin, & Clavel, 2023). Multi-modal assessment serves dual functions in phantom candidate contexts: establishing authenticity prerequisite through convergent verification across independent channels (prerequisite control), while simultaneously maintaining construct validity through multiple indicators of the same underlying competencies (psychometric requirement). This dual function distinguishes multi-modal approaches in fraud detection contexts from traditional multi-method assessment, where convergent evidence serves validity purposes exclusively. When synthetic identity threats are present, organizations must verify that authentic human referents exist before proceeding to evaluate whether assessments accurately measure job-relevant characteristics. Multi-modal assessment is also natively suited to response-process evidence: when a single underlying experience generates verbal, written, and behavioral responses in an authentic candidate, the convergence across channels is itself evidence about the cognitive processes producing the responses (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014). When that convergence breaks down, the response-process assumption itself is what fails.
Human candidates possess cognitive systems in which knowledge, memory, and expression capabilities draw on a common experiential base (Sackett et al., 2017). When authentic candidates discuss qualifications across different modalities, their responses naturally maintain consistency because all channels access identical experiential foundations. Verbal descriptions, written analyses, and behavioral demonstrations of the same competency draw from this same experiential base. Current technical research reveals that AI systems generate responses across modalities through independent computational processes that must coordinate externally rather than emerging from unified foundations (Liang, Zhu, Wu, & Chen, 2025). Real-time multi-modal synthesis faces trade-offs between speed and quality that create detectable artifacts, particularly inconsistency between visual and audio modalities, such as mismatched lip movements and speech content (Jung, Dai, Liu, & Wu, 2024). These failures occur because visual synthesis, audio generation, and content creation operate through separate computational processes that struggle with real-time coordination.
Synthesis systems exhibit measurable sensitivity to variability in audio quality, including environmental factors such as background noise, reverberation, and microphone distortion. Environmental sensitivity creates systematic vulnerabilities when AI systems must maintain coordination across modalities under variable assessment conditions. AI systems lack unified generative foundations (Schneider, Meske, & Kuss, 2024). Unlike human experiential integration, AI must coordinate separate models for language generation, visual synthesis, audio production, and behavioral simulation. Each coordination point represents a potential failure mode that compounds as modalities increase.
For authentic candidates, each additional assessment modality provides another expression channel for the same underlying competencies and experiences. The marginal difficulty of consistency maintenance remains minimal because all channels access identical knowledge foundations. For AI systems, each additional modality increases coordination complexity (Chen & Epps, 2020). When written assessments, behavioral demonstrations, and environmental variations are added, coordination requirements exceed current AI architectural capabilities. Multi-modal assessment creates detection advantages through multiplicative failure probability rather than simple additive effects. To illustrate this multiplicative principle: if we assume an AI system exhibits a 10% probability of detectable failure in any single modality, the probability of maintaining perfect coordination across three independent modalities would become 0.93 = 72.9%, yielding a 27.1% detection probability under these assumptions. This illustrative calculation demonstrates how coordination failure probability compounds as modalities increase, though actual failure rates will vary based on specific AI capabilities, implementation quality, and organizational detection thresholds. The core principle—that coordination requirements create multiplicative rather than additive detection advantages—remains valid across varying technical parameters.
Jakesch et al. (2023) support this analysis, demonstrating that AI-generated content exhibits distinctive patterns, including lower perplexity (more predictable word choices) and overly formal or polished tone that differ systematically from authentic human expression. When linguistic markers appear alongside technical synthesis artifacts, the convergent evidence provides stronger fraud detection than either signal independently (Liu & Ding, 2025). The technical foundation for multi-modal detection rests on AI architectural constraints rather than temporary implementation limitations. Separate generative models for different modalities create fundamental coordination challenges that persist even as individual model quality improves. Organizations can exploit this architectural constraint through assessment designs that require simultaneous performance across multiple channels.
Multi-modal verification thus accomplishes authenticity prerequisite establishment (detecting coordination failures indicating synthetic entities) while maintaining construct validity requirements (ensuring assessments measure authentic candidate competencies through convergent evidence). Organizations implementing multi-modal protocols establish foundational authenticity before proceeding to validity-focused evaluation of whether selection systems accurately predict job performance. High-reliability organizing principles support this integration through deference to expertise, as effective multi-modal verification requires coordination between HR professionals assessing candidate qualifications, technical specialists identifying synthesis artifacts, document authentication experts verifying credentials, and behavioral analysts detecting cross-modal inconsistencies.
Video interviews combined with written technical assessments require candidates to demonstrate competencies through both synchronous verbal communication and asynchronous written analysis. Authentic candidates can switch modalities readily because competencies derive from integrated experience. AI systems must coordinate language models for written content with real-time audio-visual synthesis, creating detectable inconsistencies in technical depth, communication style, or problem-solving approaches across modalities. Document verification integrated with behavioral assessment creates additional coordination requirements. When candidates provide credentials, employment history, and educational documentation, authentic candidates can discuss these elements naturally because documents represent their actual background. AI systems must ensure fabricated credentials align with generated interview responses and behavioral demonstrations, requiring coordination across document generation systems, conversational AI, and video synthesis that creates multiplicative failure opportunities.
High-reliability organizing principles support multi-modal integration through deference to expertise. Effective multi-modal verification requires coordination between HR professionals who assess candidate qualifications, technical specialists who identify synthesis artifacts, document authentication experts who verify credentials, and behavioral analysts who detect cross-modal inconsistencies. The organizational capability to integrate specialized knowledge across these domains enables systematic exploitation of AI coordination limitations that exceed any single function's detection capabilities. Organizations implementing multi-modal verification establish formal protocols for cross-functional review where technical specialists flag potential synthesis artifacts for HR contextualization, document experts identify credential anomalies for behavioral confirmation, and integrated analysis reveals patterns that isolated examinations miss.
The multiplicative detection advantage emerges through strategic modality selection and integration. Organizations should prioritize modalities that require different AI model types: verbal communication (language models), visual presentation (video synthesis), written analysis (text generation), behavioral demonstration (real-time coordination), and environmental adaptation (robustness testing). Each modality introduces independent technical challenges that compound when integration is required. Assessment protocols should require candidates to demonstrate consistency across modalities within short timeframes, reducing opportunities for AI systems to generate carefully coordinated responses. Real-time transitions between modalities—asking candidates to verbally explain a concept immediately after written analysis—create coordination challenges that exceed current AI capabilities while remaining natural for authentic candidates. Based on the above discussion, we propose:
Proposition 2

The capacity to detect malicious synthetic identity fraud by identifying inconsistencies across different assessment and verification modalities increases multiplicatively with the number of distinct, integrated modalities employed.
3.3. Proposition 3: Systematic learning
AI-enabled fraud operates through rapid technological evolution that continuously changes attack vectors and technical capabilities. Research documents this evolution clearly, with Kostikova et al. (2026) reviewing “14,648 relevant [LLM] limitation papers [to] identify between 7 and 15 prominent types of limitations [with] [r]easoning [as] the most studied limitation, followed by generalization, hallucination, bias, and security.” The evolution creates asymmetric learning challenges where fraud actors benefit from immediate feedback about successful attack techniques and can rapidly deploy new AI tools, while organizations often learn about fraud attempts only after detection failure, creating inherent disadvantages in the threat-defense cycle.
Malicious synthetic identity fraud requires expertise spanning HR assessment, AI technology, cybersecurity, and legal compliance. Systematic processes enable effective integration of these diverse knowledge domains, while isolated responses often lack the necessary technical expertise. Emerging research in AI safety and evaluation demonstrates that systematic benchmarking frameworks can identify recurring failure patterns in AI systems, with studies showing that standardized evaluation helps expose system limitations and coordination problems, particularly in multi-agent environments where specialized components must work together (Feng et al., 2026).
Validity arguments for selection benefit from systematic threat intelligence through continuous evidence that detection strategies establish authenticity prerequisites without compromising the relevant sources of validity evidence. Organizations must verify that assessment modifications designed to expose AI limitations preserve content-related and criterion-related evidence as those modifications evolve in response to new fraud techniques. Systematic documentation enables organizations to confirm that cognitive load variations, multi-modal requirements, and environmental adaptations successfully distinguish synthetic entities from authentic candidates while avoiding construct-irrelevant variance or adverse impact on legitimate applicants. The empirical foundation created through systematic learning enables organizations to demonstrate that authenticity verification enhances rather than compromises assessment integrity, establishing prerequisite controls that enable traditional validity frameworks to operate effectively while maintaining the psychometric rigor essential for defensible selection decisions.
Malicious synthetic identity fraud presents fundamentally different learning requirements where threats evolve through technological advancement rather than gradual environmental change, detection requires technical expertise about AI limitations that most organizations lack, and the cost of learning failure through successful fraud can be catastrophic. Organizations that systematically collect and analyze fraud attempts can identify exploitable patterns in AI technical limitations that isolated incident responses miss. Technical research reveals specific failure modes that organizations can learn to detect through systematic analysis (Rhaiem & Amara, 2021). AI sensitivity to environmental variations, and organizations that systematically test and document these sensitivities across multiple fraud attempts can develop more effective environmental stress testing, while ad hoc responses fail to accumulate this knowledge (Pandhare, 2024). Systematic threat intelligence processes enable organizations to accumulate technical knowledge about AI limitations across multiple malicious synthetic identity fraud attempts, creating several specific advantages that isolated incident responses cannot achieve. For example, multiple incidents showing slight vocal synthesis artifacts under specific acoustic conditions become recognizable patterns rather than random technical glitches when analyzed systematically. Organizations can also systematically monitor the evolution of AI fraud capabilities and corresponding detection opportunities, enabling rapid identification of new AI tools' specific limitations and vulnerabilities as they emerge.
Systematic documentation of AI failure modes enables more accurate detection because organizations develop expertise in distinguishing AI artifacts from normal technical variations or candidate nervousness, reducing false negative rates over time (Raji et al., 2020). High-reliability organizing principles support systematic learning through preoccupation with failure and institutional memory development. Organizations implementing high-reliability perspectives treat each verification episode as a learning opportunity, documenting failure modes, analyzing detection effectiveness, and updating protocols based on accumulated evidence. The preoccupation with the failure principle drives continuous questioning of whether current detection strategies remain effective against evolving AI capabilities.
Validity arguments benefit from systematic learning through continuous evidence that detection strategies maintain content-related and criterion-related validity evidence as they evolve. Organizations can verify that assessment modifications designed to expose AI limitations do not introduce bias or reduce predictive accuracy through systematic effectiveness tracking. The empirical foundation enables organizations to demonstrate that fraud detection maintains assessment integrity rather than compromising selection quality. Based on the above discussion, we propose:
Proposition 3

Organizations employing structured learning systems dedicated to capturing, analyzing, integrating, and disseminating knowledge about malicious synthetic identity fraud will exhibit significantly greater improvements over time in the effectiveness of their detection and prevention mechanisms.
3.4. Proposition 4: Human-AI integration
Current approaches to AI fraud detection in hiring contexts typically emphasize either human judgment or automated technological solutions as primary verification mechanisms (Hunkenschroer & Kriebitz, 2023; Hunkenschroer & Luetge, 2022). Human-AI integration optimizes both authenticity verification (prerequisite control through automated technical artifact detection) and psychometric validity (human contextual interpretation ensuring construct measurement remains uncontaminated by fraud detection requirements). Research on human versus AI deception detection capabilities reveals that both approaches possess distinct but complementary strengths and limitations that, when strategically integrated, create superior authenticity verification capabilities exceeding either approach operating independently.
Research by Groh, Epstein, Firestone, and Picard (2022) provides empirical evidence about human-AI deepfake detection capabilities, demonstrating that a system integrating human and model predictions is more accurate than either humans or the model alone. Research reveals that inaccurate model predictions often lead humans to incorrectly update their responses—participants became 2.7% less accurate on average when the model made incorrect predictions. The study found that humans possess particular strengths in specialized visual processing of faces and contextual reasoning. Specifically, when specialized face processing was obstructed (through video inversion, misalignment, or occlusion), human performance decreased significantly, while the AI model was largely unaffected by these manipulations. Additionally, humans demonstrated superior performance on videos of political leaders, likely due to their ability to incorporate contextual knowledge and critically evaluate whether such figures would realistically make the statements shown, while the AI model failed confidently on these same videos.
The theoretical foundation for complementary capability integration rests on documented differences between human and AI information processing that create opportunities for strategic combination. Humans excel at contextual interpretation, dynamic interaction adaptation, and multi-modal pattern recognition that enables identification of subtle inconsistencies across communication channels (Hemmer, Schemmer, Kühl, Vössing, & Satzger, 2025). Research shows that human assessors naturally focus on cross-modal consistency indicators that automated systems struggle to evaluate, particularly when fraud attempts exhibit sophisticated technical execution but subtle behavioral or contextual anomalies (Henle, Dineen, & Duffy, 2019). Human assessors then leverage automated findings to conduct targeted investigations using their superior capabilities in dynamic interaction, contextual interpretation, and adaptive questioning.
Research supports synergistic effects through evidence that optimal fraud detection requires both technical analysis and contextual interpretation. AI-generated content detection relies on identifying linguistic features like lower perplexity and greater lexical diversity that automated analysis can quantify, combined with recognizing overly formal or polished tone and lack of personal anecdotes or idiosyncratic phrasing that human assessors interpret more effectively through contextual knowledge and social intelligence (Gotoman, Luna, Sangria, Santiago Jr., & Barbuco, 2025; Terčon & Dobrovoljc, 2025).
Validity arguments benefit from human-AI integration through strengthened content-related, response-process, and criterion-related validity evidence while authenticity prerequisites are established. Automated technical analysis identifies construct-irrelevant artifacts introduced by AI synthesis without requiring human assessors to develop specialized technical expertise in deepfake detection or forensic analysis. Human judgment maintains focus on assessing authentic candidate KSAOs through contextual interpretation and adaptive questioning, while automated systems handle technical verification requirements. This division preserves assessment validity by preventing authenticity verification requirements from contaminating construct measurement—human assessors can focus cognitive resources on evaluating candidates' job-relevant competencies while technical systems establish that authentic human candidates are being assessed.
Personnel assessment maintains validity through human-AI integration by ensuring that authenticity verification enhancements do not compromise psychometric assessment objectives. Human oversight ensures that fraud detection strategies remain aligned with measuring authentic candidate qualifications rather than becoming disconnected technical exercises that introduce construct-irrelevant variance. Automated support enables human assessors to focus on validity-critical evaluation rather than technical artifact identification, preserving the assessment relationship between evaluator and candidate while technical systems operate in the background to establish foundational prerequisites. The integration creates detection capabilities superior to either approach independently because automated analysis handles computational tasks where machines demonstrate comparative advantage, while human judgment addresses adaptive and contextual challenges where human cognition excels, all while maintaining the psychometric integrity essential for valid selection decisions. Based on the above discussion, we propose:
Proposition 4

Verification systems in hiring that are designed based on socio-technical principles to augment human judgment with integrated technological tools will demonstrate significantly higher accuracy and effectiveness in detecting malicious synthetic identity fraud.
4. Discussion and conclusion
The primary theoretical contribution of this work lies in establishing authenticity verification as a prerequisite control that enables personnel assessment validity frameworks to operate when sophisticated AI-generated synthetic identities threaten to infiltrate remote hiring processes. Traditional personnel selection assumes that assessment responses originate from authentic human candidates whose future performance can be predicted—an assumption historically satisfied through physical co-presence but now vulnerable in remote contexts. The framework addresses the challenge that synthetic identity fraud poses not by introducing new sources of validity evidence requiring development, but by specifying prerequisite controls that establish the foundational condition under which the unitary validity argument can operate. Drawing upon documented AI technical limitations and high-reliability organizing principles, we develop four propositions that exploit systematic AI constraints—consistency failures, multi-modal coordination difficulties, organizational learning advantages, and human-AI complementarity—to distinguish authentic human candidates from synthetic entities while maintaining the validity evidence and reliability requirements essential for defensible selection decisions.
Traditional personnel selection assumes that candidates, whether honest or deceptive, operate through human cognitive and behavioral systems. AI-enabled fraud fundamentally alters this assumption by introducing artificial response generation that exhibits systematic technical limitations exploitable through strategic assessment design, representing a qualitative shift in selection challenges that existing theory does not adequately address. In the language of the Standards, what synthetic identity fraud most directly violates is the response-process assumption: the assumption that a candidate's responses are generated by the cognitive and behavioral processes the procedure was designed to elicit (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014). Once that assumption is in doubt, evidence based on test content and on relations to other variables cannot be interpreted in the usual way, regardless of how compelling the score appears on its face.
Proposition 1 establishes that AI systems' probabilistic reasoning architecture creates systematic consistency vulnerabilities that assessment variation can exploit, providing HRM with specific, actionable detection mechanisms based on technical rather than psychological principles. Proposition 2 demonstrates that AI coordination failures across modalities create multiplicative rather than additive detection opportunities, offering HRM professionals concrete multi-modal assessment strategies grounded in technical research rather than intuitive approaches. Proposition 3 transforms organizational learning theory by focusing specifically on threat intelligence processes designed to exploit evolving AI technical limitations, moving beyond general adaptation principles to systematic capability development for technical fraud detection. Proposition 4 advances human-AI integration theory by establishing complementary capability principles based on documented differences between human contextual intelligence and automated technical analysis, providing HRM with specific guidance for integrating technological tools effectively.
4.1. Implications for HRM practice
The theoretical framework carries significant practical implications for HRM professionals who must adapt selection practices to address malicious synthetic identity fraud. Moving beyond traditional background checking requires strategic integration of technical knowledge about AI limitations into standard assessment procedures (Parycek, Schmid, & Novak, 2024). Professional competency evolution becomes essential as HRM professionals require technical literacy about AI fraud mechanisms rather than general deception awareness (Budhwar, Malik, De Silva, & Thevisuthan, 2022). Understanding specific AI technical limitations documented in our propositions, such as consistency failures across contextual reframings, multi-modal coordination constraints, and environmental sensitivity patterns, enables HRM professionals to design assessment modifications that systematically exploit these technical limitations rather than relying on intuitive fraud detection approaches (Pan & Froese, 2023).
Assessment design changes require HRM professionals to implement specific technical strategies grounded in AI research. Consistency testing across temporal separations and contextual reframings exploits documented AI probabilistic reasoning limitations (Wright, 2025). Multi-modal assessment integration creates systematic coordination challenges for AI synthesis systems while remaining manageable for authentic candidates. Environmental variation in assessment conditions exploits AI sensitivity patterns documented in technical research. Technology integration strategy in personnel selection enables HRM professionals to leverage automated detection tools effectively through complementary capability approaches (Wright, 2025). Rather than replacing human judgment, technological integration provides technical artifact detection that guides human contextual interpretation and adaptive assessment. HRM professionals must understand how to interpret automated findings and translate technical indicators into appropriate assessment modifications.
Incident response capabilities must incorporate technical analysis protocols that enable systematic learning about AI fraud patterns rather than isolated incident management. HRM professionals need frameworks for documenting AI technical artifacts, coordinating with IT security expertise, and translating technical findings into improved assessment procedures (Tambe, Cappelli, & Yakubovich, 2019). High-reliability organizing principles require establishing cross-functional coordination structures where HR, cybersecurity, legal, and IT functions collaborate systematically rather than responding to crises reactively. Organizations implementing these practices establish standing committees with explicit responsibility for hiring security, develop shared knowledge repositories where technical intelligence informs HR practice, and create joint training programs building mutual understanding across specialized domains.
4.2. Measurement and implementation challenges
The framework's technical foundation creates specific measurement challenges that HRM research must address. Operationalizing AI technical limitations requires collaboration with computer science researchers to develop reliable measures of consistency failures, multi-modal coordination problems, and environmental sensitivity patterns that HRM professionals can apply in practice. Validation of technical assessment necessitates rigorous testing of proposed consistency challenges, multi-modal integration approaches, and environmental variation strategies (American Educational Research Association, and American Psychological Association, and National Council on Measurement in Education, 2014; Cronbach & Meehl, 1955; Kane, 2013). Developing standardized protocols for implementing technical detection strategies and measuring their effectiveness across diverse organizational contexts remains a critical research priority.
Cross-functional integration measurement presents challenges in quantifying effective collaboration between HRM professionals and technical security experts. Traditional HRM metrics may not capture the quality of technical knowledge transfer or the effectiveness of joint analysis protocols essential for systematic threat intelligence. Longitudinal adaptation tracking requires measures that capture organizational learning about evolving AI technical limitations rather than static fraud detection capabilities. Developing metrics for threat intelligence effectiveness, technical knowledge accumulation, and adaptive capability development over time presents methodological challenges requiring innovative approaches.
The framework's emphasis on technical AI limitation exploitation raises specific ethical considerations that HRM practice must address carefully. Technical assessment modifications must maintain fairness and accessibility while implementing strategies designed to challenge AI coordination capabilities. Consistency testing, multi-modal assessment, and environmental variation must not create disparate impact for human candidates while effectively detecting AI fraud. Organizations must ensure that cognitive load variations, multi-modal requirements, and environmental adaptations remain within reasonable accommodation frameworks that do not disadvantage candidates with disabilities, non-native language speakers, or those with limited technological resources.
4.3. Limitations and boundary conditions
The framework's applicability faces several constraints that define its implementation boundaries. Technology evolution sensitivity creates structural challenges where fraud actors can rapidly adopt new AI capabilities while organizations must validate and deploy countermeasures through formal processes (Bello & Olufemi, 2024). The framework's reliance on exploiting current AI limitations means each technical advance potentially invalidates existing detection strategies, creating a cycle where defensive measures consistently lag offensive capabilities. Organizations must recognize that the framework provides principles for continuous adaptation rather than static solutions, requiring ongoing investment in threat intelligence and countermeasure evolution.
Technical expertise requirements create practical barriers beyond resource constraints. The framework assumes organizations can effectively integrate computer science knowledge with HR practice, but these domains operate through different validation standards and timescales. HR professionals need immediate, reliable guidance while technical research produces probabilistic findings requiring interpretation (Tambe et al., 2019). Knowledge translation challenges limit implementation to organizations with adequate technical support or external expertise. Industry context variation affects implementation through constraints that customization cannot fully address. High-volume recruitment contexts face different feasibility requirements than selective hiring processes. When processing thousands of applications, even highly accurate detection strategies create false positive burdens that may overwhelm human verification capacity. The framework's multi-modal approaches may be economically viable only for organizations with specific risk-to-volume ratios.
Regulatory environment constraints operate through multiple interacting mechanisms. Privacy regulations limit data collection capabilities, employment law constrains assessment methods, and emerging AI governance frameworks may restrict both detection technologies and their application (Yanamala, 2024). Regulatory layers create compliance complexity that may be prohibitive for smaller organizations or those operating across multiple jurisdictions. Implementation boundaries emerge from organizational capacity and strategic priorities. Many organizations may prefer selective application based on role sensitivity rather than systematic implementation, creating exploitable gaps that sophisticated fraud operations can identify. Cost-benefit calculations vary dramatically across contexts, with organizations facing low-consequence hiring decisions potentially accepting fraud risks rather than investing in sophisticated detection capabilities.
4.4. Future research directions
Research priorities emerge from the framework's implementation challenges and theoretical gaps. Empirical validation of technical detection strategies must address the challenge that successful fraud remains undetected, creating systematic bias in validation datasets. Research necessarily studies detected fraud attempts, which may represent less sophisticated efforts than successful infiltrations. Selection bias means validation studies may overestimate detection effectiveness against the most capable threats. Researchers must develop methodologies that account for unknown successful fraud rates and validate detection strategies against increasingly sophisticated test cases that simulate well-resourced threat actors.
Measurement development confronts the challenge that fraud detection constructs must track evolving technical capabilities, unlike traditional personnel selection constructs that remain relatively stable. Measures validated against current AI limitations may lose validity as technology advances, requiring continuous revalidation cycles that exceed typical research timelines. Developing metrics for consistency testing effectiveness, multi-modal integration quality, and systematic learning capability that remain valid across technological changes requires innovative approaches. Researchers might explore construct stability assessment frameworks that identify core measurement principles robust to technical evolution while enabling operational updates as AI capabilities change.
Longitudinal studies of organizational learning face challenges that effective threat intelligence may require longer observation periods than research funding cycles typically support. Organizations may need years to develop effective learning systems, while academic research operates on shorter validation timelines. Researchers must examine how organizations develop technical threat intelligence capabilities and optimal knowledge transfer mechanisms between technical and HRM expertise. Collaborative industry-academic partnerships enabling extended observation periods while maintaining research rigor represent promising approaches. Cross-cultural and legal context research must address varying privacy expectations and institutional trust levels that affect both detection strategy acceptance and effectiveness. Verification approaches that function effectively in high-trust contexts may fail in environments where candidates expect institutional skepticism. Research must examine how the framework's technical detection strategies function across different regulatory environments and cultural contexts.
Integration with emerging AI governance requires ongoing research as AI regulation develops. Organizations must navigate evolving compliance requirements while maintaining detection effectiveness. Research must examine how AI governance frameworks affect HRM practice and fraud detection capabilities, including the interaction between privacy regulations, employment law, and AI-specific governance structures. Knowledge dissemination research must address the tension between advancing detection science and maintaining effectiveness. The research community needs strategies for advancing knowledge while preserving detection effectiveness, potentially through industry consortia, restricted circulation of operational details, or delayed publication of specific technical countermeasures.
